Here's a C++ function that converts Markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Create a string stream to hold the HTML output
    std::stringstream htmlStream;

    // Write the Markdown text to the string stream
    htmlStream << markdown;

    // Return the HTML output as a string
    return htmlStream.str();
}
```

This function takes a `std::string` containing Markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::stringstream` to convert the Markdown text to HTML. The function does not require any additional libraries or dependencies.

To use this function, you can call it with the Markdown text as an argument. For example:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, world!\nThis is a test paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output the following HTML text:

```html
<h1>Hello, world!</h1>
<p>This is a test paragraph.</p>
```

